  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Keeping quiet weather into Friday. Cold front \napproaches the region Friday night returning rain chances to the\narea for next weekend. \n\n\nCold air advection stratocu bringing mostly cloud skies to the\nmountains/foothills this afternoon, and have increased sky\ncoverage from earlier forecast. Should see skies clear out this\nevening into the overnight with overnight fog likely in the\ncooler mountain valleys.\n\nBy Tuesday, the Nor'easter and its associated upper level trough\nthat have been off the coast of New Jersey and Long Island will finally\nlift north and east of the region, which will allow for 500mb \nheights and upper level ridging to build back into the region. \nThis will return daytime highs back into the upper 70s to low \n80s for mountain locations, and upper 80s to low 90s for \nPiedmont locations starting Wednesday and continuing through the\nend of the work week. While temperatures will surge to above \nnormal values again, dewpoints look to remain in the 40s/50s \nwhich will make the airmass feel much more tolerable. \n\nBy Friday night, a broad upper level shortwave trough looks to\nslide east through the northern Great Lakes region. This will\nusher in a cold front passage late on Friday and into Saturday\nmorning, which looks to moderate temperatures back to near \nnormal to start the weekend. This frontal boundary will also\nreturn minimal rain chances to the area. Possible moisture from\na tropical system in the eastern Pacific will eventually track \ninto the southeast by early next week; however, timing and \nspecific details of how much rainfall are still uncertain at \nthis time. This moisture may bring the next best widespread \nchance for rainfall across the region.",
